\section{\jpc{}: pathwise updates through a jointly trained two-dimensional critic}
\label{sec:method}
Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism}(b) summarises the method; Algorithm~\ref{alg:jpc} gives the loop.

\paragraph{The 2D critic.}
Let $f_i(y,t,c)\in\mathbb R^{d'}$ be the per-position features of the denoiser trunk (we fine-tune a copy of $D_\theta$'s transformer at a small learning rate) applied to a noisy state. The \hl{2D critic} applies one head per position and pools afterwards,
\begin{equation}
R_i=h_\phi\big(f_i(y,t,c),t\big),\qquad V_\phi(y)=\frac1{|\mathcal G|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal G}R_i,
\label{eq:critic2d}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal G$ is the set of generated positions and $h_\phi$ is a small MLP. Its two dimensions are the flow step, since it is a function of the noisy state and its time $t$ at any step of the trajectory, and the token position, since it returns one value $R_i$ per generated position. It is pre-trained on rollouts of the base policy by binary cross-entropy of $\sigma(V_\phi)$ against the verifier outcome and then trained jointly (below). The \emph{scalar} critic of prior work pools first and applies the head afterwards, $V=h_\phi(\frac1{|\mathcal G|}\sum_i f_i,t)$. With a linear head the two coincide; with a nonlinear head they are different function classes: the 2D critic is additive over positions and its gradient $\partial V/\partial y$ reaches every position through that position's own head, whereas the scalar critic routes all positions through one pooled bottleneck. The per-position values $R_i$ are a by-product that we use for measurement (which positions a state has already committed correctly, Appendix~\ref{app:critic_maps}) and for filtering; the optimisation only uses their mean. Empirically (\S\ref{sec:ablations}) the 2D form is the one that is stable across tasks and seeds.

\paragraph{One-step pathwise update.}
Each iteration samples $B$ prompts, rolls out the deployment sampler~\eqref{eq:chain} for $K$ steps without gradients, decodes and verifies. For the policy update we draw one step index $k\sim\mathcal U\{k_0,\dots,k_1\}$ per trajectory, recompute the single transition $y_k=\alpha_kD_\theta(y_{k-1})+\sigma_k\varepsilon_k$ with gradients, and minimise $-V_\phi(y_k)$ with respect to $\theta$ with the critic held fixed. One step suffices because the critic scores a state rather than a return, and it keeps memory independent of $K$. We use the middle window $[8,40]$ of a 64-step sampler ($[4,20]$ of 32): the earliest steps are pure noise and the last ones are already committed.

\paragraph{Joint training.}
Rollouts (states at several $k$, their outcomes) are pushed into a replay buffer of $N$ trajectories; after every policy step the critic takes $m$ gradient steps on minibatches from the buffer with the BCE loss on the verifier outcome. The critic therefore sees the current policy's states within one iteration, which is what removes the exploit: with the critic frozen at its pre-trained weights, the same update raises $V_\phi$ while the true success stays flat or falls (\S\ref{sec:ablations}). We found $m{=}2$ and $N$ between $4$k and $16$k trajectories to be the stable range; $m{=}1$ is too weak and $m{=}8$ over-fits the buffer. Actor and critic are checkpointed together, so that a run can be resumed or refined at a lower learning rate without a cold critic.

\paragraph{Deployment sampler and early stopping.}
Gains lock to the sampler they were trained under: a policy trained at $\gamma4@16$ improves $\gamma4@16$ and barely moves $\gamma56@64$ (\S\ref{sec:ablations}), so training runs under the sampler that will be deployed. Every run, on every task, eventually enters a regime in which the critic's value keeps rising while the true success rate falls (Figure~\ref{fig:curves}); a smaller learning rate delays this without removing it. We therefore evaluate checkpoints on held-out prompts and keep the peak. On Game24 the replay-buffer success rate is \emph{not} a proxy for held-out accuracy (training sets versus unseen sets), so the held-out evaluation is part of the recipe.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{\jpc{} (one round of joint training)}
\label{alg:jpc}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE Pre-train the 2D critic $\phi$ on base-policy rollouts (BCE on verifier outcome).
\FOR{iteration $=1,\dots,T$}
  \STATE Roll out $B$ prompts with the deployment sampler~\eqref{eq:chain}; decode; verify $r$.
  \STATE Push $(y_k,t_k,c,r)$ for $k$ in the stored set into the replay buffer (capacity $N$).
  \STATE Draw one $k$ per trajectory; recompute $y_k$ with gradients; $\theta\leftarrow\theta-\eta\,\nabla_\theta\big[-V_\phi(y_k)\big]$.
  \STATE Take $m$ critic steps on buffer minibatches: $\phi\leftarrow\phi-\eta_\phi\nabla_\phi\,\mathrm{BCE}\big(\sigma(V_\phi),r\big)$.
  \STATE Every $S$ iterations: checkpoint $(\theta,\phi)$; evaluate on held-out prompts; keep the best.
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig1_mechanism.pdf}
\caption{(a) A reward bit is attached to $\sim$30 categorical decisions in an AR model and spread over $10^{5}$--$10^{6}$ Gaussian noise coordinates in a flow language model; the score of a Gaussian transition is the noise itself. The pathwise gradient replaces it with a deterministic per-coordinate signal from a critic on noisy states. (b) \jpc{}: the 2D critic scores every position of a random intermediate state; its pooled value is the actor's objective, its per-position values are a by-product used for measurement and filtering, and it is trained on the same rollouts as the policy.}
\label{fig:mechanism}
\end{figure}
